  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through mid week.\n\nThe latest surface analysis paints a quiet picture over the area\nwith sprawling high pressure extending over the Great Lakes into\nthe Mid-Atlantic states. Conditions remain tranquil this evening\nwith just some passing mid to high clouds dropping down from \nthe upper Mid-Atlantic. The dry nature of the air mass will \nsupport ample cooling into the night.\n\nHigh pressure will persist over the Mid-Atlantic through Wednesday\nbefore gradually moving offshore. Large scale subsidence and \ndry air lead to a precipitation-free forecast with temperatures \nrising to several degrees above normal as the high pulls \noffshore. This leads to high temperatures in the mid to upper \n80s Tuesday, perhaps near 90 Wednesday (slightly cooler in the \nmountains). Low temperatures (and dew points) will generally be \nin the 50s and 60s during this time. Some river valley fog is \nlikely each morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will begin to flatten out Wednesday before \nbreaking down and pushing south and east into Thursday. Several \npieces of upper-level energy rotating around an upper low \ncentered over the Hudson Bay will slowly push a cold front south\ntowards our region by Thursday. At the surface, high pressure \npushing offshore will bring increasingly warm and humid \nconditions to the area, with highs in the 80s to near 90 with \ndew points in the upper 60s to low 70s, with the highest values \nbeing Thursday.\n\nModel guidance over the past 24 hours has come into better agreement \nin regards to the progression of the front on Thursday, as well as \nthe upper-level pattern in association with it. The front \nreally looks like it will flatten out as it moves southward, and\nreally loses the upper-level support. The timing of peak \nheating and the highest shear just doesn't look to line up at \nthis point. There may be some jet-induced showers and storms \nlate Thursday as we move into the right entrance of the upper \njet centered over New England, but not necessarily expecting \nthose to be severe at that point, as the front should be largely\nthrough the region. At this point, the severe weather threat \nlooks very limited, but any storm that develops on the warm side\nof the boundary could bring some gusty winds along with it, but\nthe lack of a substantial wind field will really make that more\ndifficult with southward extent.\n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore Friday, temps drop again\nand conditions become much drier heading into the start of the \nweekend as high pressure moves in overhead. The next system may\napproach by late next weekend.",
